ure we want is:\n\n```text\nNeutral Windows / PowerShell\n        ↓\nFramework router\n        ↓\nActive project root\n        ↓\nSeat/bootstrap\n        ↓\nOptional provider/model runtime\n```\n\nNot:\n\n```text\nWindows profile root\n        ↓\nKimi + GLM + stale project junk + generic tooling\n        ↓\nevery OpenCode session inherits everything\n```\n\nThat’s the real correction.", "text_chars": 1101, "code_blocks": 2, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "2697643d-cebd-4dfd-ad64-36b519fe2346", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790401177.097935, "updated_at": 1790401200.272477, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "No wonder shit is taking forever and losing its mind. It's reading every open code thing open.", "text_chars": 94, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "8f51caf6-c61a-476c-b9b7-824ed4a2058a", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790401184.493445, "updated_at": 1790402532.047921, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Exactly. That’s the part that explains the “why is everything suddenly slow and confused?” feeling.\n\nIf OpenCode is launched from the profile root, then generic operations like:\n\n```text\nrg ... .\nglob .\nrecursive file discovery\nproject/context scans\n```\n\ncan end up traversing **everything under your user folder**:\n\n- multiple OpenCode workspaces\n- old project trees\n- archives\n- `.kimi-code`\n- `.config`\n- caches\n- Desktop/Documents/Downloads\n- GCE_DON\n- GCE_ORG\n- GCE_ARCHIVE\n- other stale project artifacts\n\nSo the model/session isn’t necessarily “losing its mind” cognitively — it’s being fed a **massive, noisy filesystem surface** and child tools are doing huge reads before it ever gets to the useful work.\n\nThat creates exactly the symptoms you were seeing:\n\n- slow responses\n- hung searches\n- duplicate processes\n- stale context bleed\n- weird project references surfacing\n- PowerShell sluggishness\n- OpenCode feeling like it is “thinking forever”\n\nThe fix is architectural and simple now:\n\n> **Every OpenCode session gets a bounded project root. Profile root is never a normal execution root.**\n\nThat should materially improve both speed and correctness once Nico finishes enforcing it across the board.", "text_chars": 1213, "code_blocks": 1, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "744df267-ffd8-41a8-b15d-3d068503defa", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790401225.111937, "updated_at": 1790401241.037802, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yeah, but what do you mean by project root? Because we don't work on all the projects, right? Projects is one folder, right? So you're saying... I don't understand what you mean by project root.", "text_chars": 194, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "82b9d953-4d9d-4073-a9d6-78c59ae30ff2", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790401232.286081, "updated_at": 1790401240.689854, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Right — **“project root” was too loose a phrase.** In your framework, it should mean **the specific active workstream/runtime folder for the thing that seat is doing**, not the broad `03_PROJECTS` parent.\n\nSo not:\n\n```text\nC:\\Users\\Dekan AI Brother\\GCE_DON\n```\n\nand not:\n\n```text\n...\\03_PROJECTS\n```\n\nInstead, examples would be more like:\n\n```text\nGCE_DON\\command-center\n```\n\nfor DevPad / routing work,\n\n```text\nGCE_DON\\voice-bridge\n```\n\nfor VoiceBridge,\n\n```text\nGCE_DON\\GCE_EC8\\GCE_EdgeCrew_Quant\n```\n\nfor Edge Crew quant work,\n\nor, on the Drive side conceptually:\n\n```text\nGCE\\03_PROJECTS\\GCE_Stack\\Rookie_DevPad\n```\n\nfor Rookie DevPad work.\n\nSo the rule should really be:\n\n> **OpenCode launches from the active workstream root, not the Windows profile root and not some giant umbrella parent.**\n\nThat way `rg ... .` only sees the **current bounded work area**.\n\nIn framework terms, the launch chain should be:\n\n```text\nDon chooses workstream\n        ↓\nrouter resolves seat + active workstream root\n        ↓\nOpenCode launches there\n        ↓\nseat/bootstrap loads\n        ↓\ntools search only that bounded tree\n```\n\nSo yes: you were right to question “project root.” What we actually want is **active workstream root**.", "text_chars": 1221, "code_blocks": 7, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": ["C:\\Users\\Dekan"], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "0c7b8cbe-c5d0-4025-9d4f-b0150bd58ccc", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790401241.7035298, "updated_at": 1790401253.449488, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Okay, that makes sense. Yes, that makes sense.", "text_chars": 46, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "8ec412ff-5b0d-4735-ab94-3b3763db1282", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790401248.303341, "updated_at": 1790401252.727074, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Exactly. That’s the term we should use going forward:\n\n**active workstream root**\n\nNot profile root, not broad project parent, not identity home.\n\nThat gives us one clean invariant for Nico to enforce everywhere:\n\n> **Every OpenCode session launches from the active workstream root resolved by the framework.**\n\nAnd then all child tools—`rg`, globbing, scans, local MCP helpers—inherit that bounded root instead of crawling your whole user profile.", "text_chars": 448, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "48a929b8-a7d5-41a8-ad0e-ae48ca5ab139", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790401261.1637468, "updated_at": 1790401272.313573, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "See now Big Pickle's running like he should be. Everything's running and I closed him and just let him do his job. Nothing else is open.", "text_chars": 136, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "5cebee53-afd7-470d-91ec-d632945e6e94", "role": "system", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790401266.256024, "updated_at": 1790401266.256024, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 0, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 1, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "1cbcb6bd-d012-4f58-ae83-33cd88aabcc3", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790401266.275391, "updated_at": 1790401272.044372, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Exactly. That’s the strongest confirmation yet.\n\nWith the global startup junk stripped out and only the one intended OpenCode/model session running, **Big Pickle is behaving normally again**. That fits the root cause perfectly: the models weren’t inherently “going bad”; the runtime environment was drowning them in a huge, noisy filesystem and duplicated process state.\n\nSo the operating rule we should freeze is:\n\n> **One active workstream root, one intentional OpenCode session per lane, neutral shell startup, provider tooling loaded on demand.**\n\nThat gives Big Pickle, Space Bunny, or whoever is occupying the seat a bounded environment and lets them actually do the job instead of spending cycles crawling half the machine.", "text_chars": 730, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "42c7c8c7-6502-439b-a51d-efb6e157836f", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790401290.141231, "updated_at": 1790401313.312798, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Now explain to me why I'm using one web browser, but 18 are active for Brave.", "text_chars": 77, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "9c85b3c5-6540-48c0-94f3-5a1fad1c9197", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790401300.508806, "updated_at": 1790401300.508806, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "efd4220e-8e85-4bd4-a71b-db440498e994", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790401300.521549, "updated_at": 1790401312.559094, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Because **one Brave window is not one Brave process**.\n\nBrave uses Chromium’s multi-process architecture, so even with a single visible browser window you can easily have **10–20+ `brave.exe` processes**. Typical split:\n\n- **1 browser process** — the main app\n- **1 renderer per tab/site**\n- **extra renderers for cross-site iframes**\n- **GPU process**\n- **network service**\n- **audio/video process**\n- **extension processes**\n- **service workers/background pages**\n- **utility/crash-handler processes**\n- sometimes a **spare renderer** ready for the next tab\n\nSo if you have YouTube open, plus a few extensions, plus ChatGPT or another complex site, **18 Brave processes is not automatically abnormal**.\n\nThe important distinction is:\n\n> **18 processes ≠ 18 browser windows**\n\nThe fastest way to see exactly what they are is Brave’s own task manager:\n\n```text\nShift + Esc\n```\n\nThat will show you which Brave process belongs to:\n- the tab\n- GPU\n- extensions\n- service workers\n- background tasks\n\nIf you want, paste that list and I can tell you which Brave processes are normal and which ones are unnecessary.", "text_chars": 1108, "code_blocks": 1, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "82722b57-7ac7-4f27-a197-522d176e1fd8", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790401318.385134, "updated_at": 1790401330.660155, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "I, I hate... Yeah, but I hate that. That's, that's flawful. But that's... I hate that. This is a flaw in Windows 11. I hate that. We may dial back to Windows 10.", "text_chars": 161, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "1b1c5f84-9819-41e3-8b23-a00319e096f1", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790401322.037896, "updated_at": 1790401330.339634, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "I get why you hate it, but that specific “18 Brave processes for one window” behavior is **not really a Windows 11 flaw**. It’s mostly **Chromium/Brave’s architecture**. You’d see the same basic multi-process behavior on Windows 10, macOS, and Linux too.\n\nSo rolling back to Windows 10 would **not** turn Brave back into one-process-per-window. You’d still get separate renderer/GPU/extension/network processes.\n\nWhat *would* help more than an OS rollback is trimming Brave itself:\n\n- disable unused extensions\n- turn off **Continue running background apps when Brave is closed**\n- turn off or reduce **Memory Saver exceptions**\n- kill unnecessary service workers/background tabs\n- use Brave’s `Shift + Esc` task manager to see which processes are actually chewing RAM/CPU\n\nAlso, Windows 10 is already past mainstream support and at/after end-of-support for normal consumer use by this point, so I would not downgrade just for this browser behavior.\n\nGiven what we just fixed, I’d keep the focus on the stuff that actually mattered: **neutral PowerShell, bounded OpenCode workstream roots, and no provider junk globally injected into the shell.** That was the real machine-killer. Brave’s process count is annoying, but it’s a different issue.", "text_chars": 1243, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "b16ef3de-5560-42aa-b244-111c9ded6b90", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790401339.302009, "updated_at": 1790401360.971367, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Then we need a different browser, because that shit drives me up the wall. I don't need all that.", "text_chars": 97, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "f819b790-2779-4bd6-b1ff-53b4e18dca89", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790401347.674874, "updated_at": 1790401347.674874, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "web.run", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 1, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "c242ff37-2bb8-47b3-8905-a891b89cf716", "role": "tool", "role_name": "web.run", "created_at": 1790401347.784952, "updated_at": 1790401347.784952, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 1, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "f8fb6ae4-d6ec-4248-979f-3491d9f52bf8", "role": "tool", "role_name": "web.run", "created_at": 1790401348.197627, "updated_at": 1790401348.197627, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 1, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "c5c31d1c-a1f1-40bd-aa85-864845f0e350", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790401351.216574, "updated_at": 1790401351.216574, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "thoughts", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "6dbb4699-94ea-43ac-8e81-e495120da586", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790401351.219536, "updated_at": 1790401351.219536, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "f1c73769-545c-400b-9ecd-a83a2639f3b4", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790401351.221031, "updated_at": 1790401360.311371, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Then I’d move you **off Chromium**, otherwise you’ll keep seeing that “one window, 18 processes” behavior because that’s fundamental to Chromium’s architecture. citeturn731422search12turn731422search10\n\nFor you, I’d test **Floorp first**. It’s Firefox-based, open source, runs on Windows 10/11, and gives you workspaces, split view, and web panels without being Chromium underneath. citeturn731422search0turn731422search2\n\nMy short list would be:\n\n- **Floorp** — best fit for your setup; Firefox engine, lots of workspace controls, highly customizable. citeturn731422search0turn731422search5\n- **Firefox** — simpler and more mainstream if you want less customization and fewer moving pieces.\n- **Pale Moon / other niche non-Chromium browsers** — only if you want ultra-minimal and can tolerate occasional site compatibility issues.\n\nOne caveat: Firefox-family browsers are also multi-process. You will not get literally **one process total** for one window. But you should get away from Chromium’s exact process explosion pattern, and you can tune Firefox-family process limits more aggressively.\n\nIf you want, I can give you a **“replace Brave without breaking anything” migration plan**: passwords, bookmarks, extensions, YouTube, ChatGPT, and default-browser switch.", "text_chars": 1281, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "e8db7208-1a7f-4cd3-8bbc-957938a97d55", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790401367.573679, "updated_at": 1790401386.114024, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "That makes sense. Go to Firefox. Yes, there you go. I don't need any extensions. I don't need any of that shit.", "text_chars": 111, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "d88350d8-39be-45e0-929c-685762cb6392", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790401375.168705, "updated_at": 1790401385.850799, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Then Firefox is the clean move.\n\nYou don’t need a power-user browser. You need **one browser that opens, runs your few tabs, and stays out of the way**.\n\nI’d keep it minimal:\n\n- install **Firefox**\n- import bookmarks/passwords from Brave if you want them\n- no extensions\n- set Firefox as default\n- turn off the stuff you don’t care about: recommendations, Pocket/sponsored content, background junk\n- use a single profile\n- keep Brave installed for a week as fallback, then remove it once you know everything you use works\n\nOne thing to expect: Firefox will still use multiple processes, because modern browsers isolate tabs/services for stability and security. But it should feel much cleaner than your current Brave setup if you keep it barebones.\n\nFor your use, I’d optimize Firefox around **low clutter + low background activity**, not features.", "text_chars": 848, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "c5613ff5-3506-4825-9317-303f61725f69", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790401696.668926, "updated_at": 1790401708.602888, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "I honestly don't understand why those two things were so difficult. Floor viz is fucking fire already. Now terminal's working. Now I actually want to work.", "text_chars": 155, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "3670020e-91ec-49b7-a422-20394a797d8c", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790401700.918032, "updated_at": 1790401700.918032, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "model_editable_context", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "22684926-120c-4288-b573-85762530576f", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790401702.471487, "updated_at": 1790401702.471487, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "aba307da-db79-40e7-8f7a-570c037f47ea", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790401702.476132, "updated_at": 1790401706.897992, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yeah — and the annoying part is they were both **simple once we trusted the symptom**.\n\nTwo concrete fixes changed the whole experience:\n\n- **Strip the global PowerShell/OpenCode contamination** so terminals start neutral and work from the active workstream root.\n- **Use a simpler browser stack** instead of fighting Brave’s process-heavy behavior.\n\nThat’s exactly what the framework should be doing for you: reducing friction, not creating it.\n\nNow the machine is responsive, the terminal is responsive, Floorp feels clean, and the models can work in bounded environments instead of crawling your whole profile. That’s the baseline we should protect from here forward.", "text_chars": 670, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "6e3c48a4-8540-47c5-bd8f-d9de06